      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(channels): honor configured read target policies",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where delegated channel actions could read or mutate conversations outside the active conversation and configured target policy. The affected paths included message, reaction, pin, member, search, information, and provider-tool actions across bundled and external channel plugins.\n\nThe correction also needed to preserve valid operator workflows: direct authenticated operations, exact-current provider aliases, targetless actions that do not read a conversation, and supported account-specific behavior.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis enforces conversation-read policy at the owning channel action boundary before prohibited provider access. Invocation authority is operation-local, derived by the host at trusted ingress, and defaults to delegated when missing or unknown.\n\n- Delegated model, agent, MCP, Codex, Copilot, skill, child-agent, and resumed-session calls remain limited to the trusted current conversation or configured targets.\n- Direct authenticated operator actions retain cross-conversation behavior without bypassing account selection, channel configuration, provider permissions, action allowlists, approvals, transport validation, or SSRF protection.\n- Bundled plugins use their provider-owned authorization. External and legacy registrations receive conservative host-owned exact-current enforcement and cannot self-attest broader authority.\n- Provider metadata and content needed for authorization are fetched only after preliminary target and account checks.\n- Invocation authority is not stored on connections, sessions, transcripts, configuration, plugin descriptors, or executable caches.\n\nAdjacent provider corrections include Feishu read-before-authorization and reaction ownership, Matrix pin authorization and account isolation, Discord DM/category policy, Slack MPIM classification, Microsoft Teams guarded transport and action identity, Google Chat outbound capability alignment, and exact-current compatibility for iMessage, Telegram, Nextcloud Talk, and external channel plugins.\n\n## User Impact\n\nModel- and agent-triggered channel reads now honor the active account and current-or-configured conversation policy consistently. Blocked, stale, wrong-account, invalid-account, and target-kind-confused requests fail before prohibited provider reads or mutations.\n\nDirect operator workflows, valid current-conversation aliases, configured cross-conversation actions, inbound attachments, threads, and provider-supported sibling actions continue to work. Google Chat service-account delivery remains text-only while inbound text, threads, and attachments remain supported.\n\n## Evidence\n\nFull live-matrix candidate: `c583dd32e1461d611a09d74d6a4401aacea467d9`  \nCurrent PR HEAD: `d02898c46e230dbe9ce8d8286c0a5e1c4999e930`\n\n**Release gate:** 89 scenario proofs and all four validation gates passed, with zero product failures or external blockers.\n\n### Live provider coverage\n\n| Provider | Live coverage |\n| --- | --- |\n| Discord | Channel, thread, DM, group-DM, category inheritance, reads, search, reactions, pins, metadata, and disabled-target enforcement |\n| Slack | Public/private channels, DM, MPIM, threads, reads, reactions, files, pins, and current/configured/blocked targets |\n| Feishu/Lark | DM/group reads, chat/member/directory tools, reaction ownership and clear-all, account isolation, interactive cards, callbacks, and reject-before-read behavior |\n| Google Chat | DM/group/thread routing, real inbound attachments, media fallback, placeholder cleanup, and pre-provider rejection of unsupported outbound actions |\n| Matrix | DM/group/thread topology, cold start, aliases, polls, room/member information, encryption/verification reads, pins, and blocked-target enforcement |\n| Microsoft Teams | Chat/channel topology, OAuth refresh, Graph transport, reads, search, pagination, reactions, files, edits, deletes, participants, and inbound callbacks on Node 22 and 24 |\n| iMessage | Every supported current-conversation alias and ID-only action form, driven by a real model turn and verified provider-side |\n| Telegram | Real inbound sticker, production cache population, model-selected targetless sticker search, and visible reply |\n| Nextcloud Talk | All five equivalent current-room forms through real webhook, model-selected delegated actions, and provider-side verification |\n\n### Cross-cutting coverage\n\n- Direct-operator and delegated/model authority across HTTP, Gateway RPC, MCP, Codex, Copilot, skills, child agents, and resumed sessions.\n- Current, configured, blocked, stale, wrong-account, invalid-account, disabled-account, and nonexistent-account cases.\n- Target normalization and semantic target-kind separation across provider aliases, IDs, DMs, groups, channels, and threads.\n- Direct/delegated cache ordering, concurrent mixed-authority calls, idempotency partitioning, transcript isolation, capability expiry, revocation, and replay rejection.\n- Stable `2026.6.11` upgraded in place to the candidate with preserved state, plugin loading, delegated resumed-session execution, and restart validation.\n- Old/new Gateway version skew and previous-plugin compatibility, including fail-closed behavior before prohibited provider reads.\n\n### Deterministic validation\n\n- 645 focused tests passed.\n- 28 broad changed-test shards passed.\n- Changed checks, production build, package inventory, digest verification, and mandatory autoreview passed.\n- The final protocol/SDK synchronization delta from the full live-matrix candidate passed an additional 323 focused tests, production build, protocol generation check, and all branch-related CI checks.\n",
